depfinder
George Vlahavas
%%mtime(%m/%d/%Y)

%!target: man
%!encoding: utf-8

= NAME =

depfinder - a tool that finds dependencies of slackware packages


= SYNOPSIS =

**depfinder** [//OPTIONS//] //PACKAGE// or //PACKAGING-DIRECTORY//


= DESCRIPTION =

**depfinder** is a tool that finds the dependencies of Slackware packages.
It examines binary files in the packages and the libraries they link to and
reports the packages these libraries belong to in a dependency list.
The dependencies are by default being output to a comma separated
list without version information, but there are options to output
dependencies with version information and to a file: a .dep file
with a comma separated list without version information, or a
//slack-required// file that includes version information.

**depfinder** can check //.tgz//, //.tbz//, //.tlz//, //.txz// and
//.tbr// packages for dependencies
but you can also point it to a packaging directory where you have the
package contents extracted.

**depfinder** uses **ldd** for discovering dependencies, and finds
dependencies of binary files with it. Detecting python dependencies is
also  supported by use of the **-p** switch.

Note that **ldd** may run code from the files it examines, so only use
**depfinder** on packages you trust, such as the ones you build
yourself.

If a certain library is included in more than one packages, then all
packages that include it will be listed in one entry using an "or"
statement (which is expressed with the pipe symbol: "|"). For example a
dependency for a library that is included in both //openssl// and
//openssl-solibs// packages will be output as:
//openssl|openssl-solibs//.

You need to have all required dependencies for any package you run
**depfinder** on installed in your system. That will of course always be
the case when you are building your own packages. In case there is a
library that is required by a package you run **depfinder** on that is
missing from your system, **depfinder** will report the missing library
and will not generate a dependency list.

If a library that a package needs is found in the system, but no
installed package provides it (for example, it was installed by hand
in ///usr/local//), **depfinder** displays a warning, since it can't
add a package for it to the dependency list. The same goes for python
modules with **-p**.

If a python module that is needed by a package is not present in the
system, a warning will be displayed instead of an error and the
dependency list will be generated. Consider these warnings as hints for
missing dependencies, optional or not. If your python application doesn't
work, it's probably because of one or more of those reported missing
modules. If your application seems to work fine, but you still get some
warnings, it might mean that your application might acquire some extra
functionality by installing some of the reported modules. And as
**depfinder** can't know which parts of the code will actually run,
there might be some false positives in these warnings, for example for
a module that is imported only on other operating systems. See **-p**
for the details.

**depfinder** also has support for running multiple jobs, which can
make it somewhat faster for large packages on PCs with multiple
CPUs/cores.


= OPTIONS =

: **-f**
Outputs dependencies to a file in the current directory instead of
stdout. By default it creates a .dep file with a comma separated list
of dependencies without version information, named after the package
file (e.g. //foo-1.0-x86_64-1.dep//), or for a packaging directory
after the package named in its //install/slack-desc// (e.g.
//foo.dep//). See also **-s**.

: **-s**
Outputs dependencies including version information. Dependencies will be
listed in the format following this example: "zlib >= 1.2.3-i486-2". If
combined with **-f**, it creates a slack-required file which can be
placed inside the package under the //install// directory.

: **-a**
Checks all files in the directory tree instead of following the FHS. If
**-a** is not used, then depfinder looks for binary files only under
///sbin//, ///usr/sbin//, ///usr/bin//, ///bin//, ///usr/libexec//,
///usr/games//, ///usr/lib//, ///lib// and, on 64-bit architectures,
///usr/lib64// and ///lib64//. If **-a** is used, then depfinder
searches for files dynamically linking to libraries anywhere inside the
package. This option could be usefull if the installation scripts put
binaries in a non-standard location.

: **-p**
Attempts to discover python dependencies. Using this switch, depfinder
searches for all files that can be recognized as python scripts
(files ending in //.py// and files starting with a python //#!// line).
They are parsed with python's own parser (nothing in the package is
run) for the modules they import, and each module that is not in the
package itself is looked up the way python finds it, in the python
3 module path of the system (modules in the user's home directory are
not used). depfinder then reports which packages these modules
belong to along with all other detected dependencies. Python
distributions required in the package's python metadata
(//Requires-Dist// in *.dist-info/METADATA, or *.egg-info/requires.txt)
are reported too, except the ones only needed for extras or for other
python versions. Python files and metadata in a directory for another
python version than the system's python 3 (e.g.
///usr/lib64/python2.7//) are not checked, with a warning. So are
scripts without a //.py// extension whose //#!// line runs them with
another python version; a plain //python// (///usr/bin/python// or
//#!/usr/bin/env python//) counts as python 2. The //#!// line of
//.py// files is ignored, since many python 3 modules still have an
old //#!/usr/bin/env python// line.

Modules that can't be found in the system are reported as warnings.
Modules imported only inside a //try// block that catches //ImportError//
are optional, since the code is written to work without them: they are
not added as dependencies, but listed in a note, as candidates for
suggested packages (except modules from python's standard library).
Imports in the //except ImportError:// block are fallbacks, which are
only needed if something imported in the //try// block can't be found:
after //try: import queue//, an //import Queue// for python 2 is
ignored, but after //try: import tomllib// (python 3.11), an
//import tomli// is needed on older pythons.
Imports under //if TYPE_CHECKING:// are ignored, and so are imports in
branches for python 2 (or other versions), when the condition is a
simple check of the python version (//sys.version_info//,
//sys.hexversion//, //PY2//, //PY3//), and imports for other platforms
(//sys.platform//, //os.name//, //platform.system()//). Modules of
python's standard library on other platforms (e.g. //msvcrt// on
Windows) are never reported. So are test files
(in //test// or //tests// directories, //test_*.py//, //*_test.py// and
//conftest.py//), since their imports are only needed to run the
tests, and so is documentation (in ///usr/doc// and ///usr/share/doc//), such as examples. Files that are not
valid python 3 are reported and skipped.

Python itself is reported too: the system's python 3 if any python 3
file was checked, and the package of ///usr/bin/python2.7// for files
for python 2, which are not checked.

The list of reported python dependencies might still not be final.
Some python packages might be reported as dependencies, while actually
being optional dependencies, for example when an //import// is only
executed after some certain action is taken by the end user, and
modules imported by a name computed at run time can't be detected at
all. It's up to the packager to determine which of the reported python
dependencies are actually dependencies and which ones should merely be
suggested packages.

**-p** needs python 3.

: **-j** //jobs//
Specifies the number of parallel depfinder jobs to run. It can help
with large packages and is best set to the number of your CPUs/cores
(default number of jobs: 1)


= GENERAL OPTIONS =

: **-v**
Shows version information for depfinder and exits.

: **-h**
Prints a usage message briefly summarizing all command-line options
and exits.


= ENVIRONMENT =

: **DEPFINDERPKGDB**
The directory holding the database of installed packages, which is used
to find which package provides each library. The default is
///var/lib/pkgtools/packages//.

: **TMPDIR**
The directory where **depfinder** creates its temporary working
directory, into which a package is extracted. The default is ///tmp//.


= WEBSITE =

http://depfinder.sourceforge.net/


= AUTHORS =

depfinder was written by George Vlahavas <vlahavas~at~gmail~dot~com>


= SEE ALSO =

**ldd**(1)
